 "fieldData": {
  "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Volunteer Form Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is a volunteer form?\", \"a\": \"A volunteer form collects what an organization needs before someone helps out: contact details, availability, the roles they want, and any useful skills. A one-day event can get by with a short volunteer sign up form. Ongoing roles usually call for a longer application.\"}, {\"q\": \"How can I create a volunteer form?\", \"a\": \"List the roles you need filled and what each one requires, then describe them to Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder'>AI form builder</a>. It builds the form, the questions that change by role, and the table that holds every volunteer. When a program adds a role, ask for the change in plain words.\"}, {\"q\": \"When should a volunteer sign up form include a waiver?\", \"a\": \"Include one when the shift carries physical risk, such as a trail crew or a home build. A waiver is a release: the volunteer accepts the risks of the activity and gives up certain claims against the organization if they get hurt. A parent or guardian usually signs for anyone under 18. State law decides how far a waiver holds up, and a lawyer should review the wording.\"}, {\"q\": \"How to fill out a volunteer form?\", \"a\": \"Answer every required field and be exact about availability. \\\"Weekday evenings after 6:00 PM\\\" tells a coordinator far more than \\\"flexible\\\" does. Choose only roles you can commit to. List skills that seem minor, since a coordinator may be looking for a Spanish speaker or someone with a truck.\"}, {\"q\": \"What questions go on a volunteer application form?\", \"a\": \"Most programs need contact details, the role applied for, availability, related experience, and two references. Roles that involve minors add an emergency contact and a background check form. Ask about a license only when the role needs one, such as driving the food pantry van.\"}, {\"q\": \"How many fields should a volunteer sign up form have?\", \"a\": \"Five or six fields cover a single event: name, email, phone, the shift or task, and a box for anyone under 18. Each extra question costs signups at a busy registration table. An Emergent form has no response cap, and a cleanup that draws 400 people stays open until the coordinator closes it.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I review a volunteer application form?\", \"a\": \"Read each one against the requirements of the role it names, then contact the references. Roles with minors add a background check before anyone is placed, and a short orientation call comes last. On Emergent, every application sits in a database you own, with a status column that shows the step each applicant has reached.\"}, {\"q\": \"What should a volunteer form for students include?\", \"a\": \"Beyond name and grade, a student form needs a parent or guardian contact for anyone under 18. Record the organization and the hours served with a short description of the work. Leave room for the site supervisor to sign and add a phone number.\"}, {\"q\": \"Where can I find a free volunteer form template?\", \"a\": \"Template galleries offer free volunteer forms as PDF and Word files. A printable volunteer form works for a one-day event with a clipboard at the door. For an ongoing program, an online form saves retyping every answer, and Emergent builds one free from a description of your roles.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is a volunteer signup form?\", \"a\": \"It is the short form people fill in to claim a spot at one event, usually contact details and the time or task they pick. It skips references and screening. A general <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder/sign-up-form'>sign up form</a> covers the same pattern for any event with limited places.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
 }
}